<p class="lt-chem-38322">Sometimes, the Latin name of the metal is used when the English name is clumsy. For example, <em>ferrate</em> is used instead of <em>ironate</em>, <em>plumbate</em> instead <em>leadate</em>, and <em>stannate</em> instead of <em>tinate</em>. The oxidation state of the metal is determined based on the charges of each ligand and the overall charge of the coordination compound. For example, in [Cr(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>4</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>]Br, the coordination sphere (in brackets) has a charge of 1+ to balance the bromide ion. The water ligands are neutral, and the chloride ligands are anionic with a charge of 1&minus; each. To determine the oxidation state of the metal, we set the overall charge equal to the sum of the ligands and the metal: +1 = &minus;2 + <em>x</em>, so the oxidation state (<em>x</em>) is equal to 3+.</p>



<section class="box-example">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chemistry_1e_(OpenSTAX)/19:_Transition_Metals_and_Coordination_Chemistry/19.02:_Coordination_Chemistry_of_Transition_Metals" class="mt-section"><span id="Example_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B1.7D.5C):_Coordination_Numbers_and_Oxidation_States"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-38322 editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Example \(\PageIndex{1}\): <span>Coordination Numbers and Oxidation States</span></span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-38322">Determine the name of the following complexes and give the coordination number of the central metal atom.</p>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
    <li class="lt-chem-38322">Na<sub>2</sub>[PtCl<sub>6</sub>]</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-38322">K<sub>3</sub>[Fe(C<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub>]</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-38322">[Co(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>5</sub>Cl]Cl<sub>2</sub></li>
</ol>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chemistry_1e_(OpenSTAX)/19:_Transition_Metals_and_Coordination_Chemistry/19.02:_Coordination_Chemistry_of_Transition_Metals" class="mt-section"><span id="Solution"></span><h6 class="editable"><span>Solution</span></h6>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
    <li class="lt-chem-38322">There are two Na<sup>+</sup> ions, so the coordination sphere has a negative two charge: [PtCl<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2&minus;</sup>. There are six anionic chloride ligands, so &minus;2 = &minus;6 + <em>x</em>, and the oxidation state of the platinum is 4+. The name of the complex is sodium hexachloroplatinate(IV), and the coordination number is six.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-38322">The coordination sphere has a charge of 3&minus; (based on the potassium) and the oxalate ligands each have a charge of 2&minus;, so the metal oxidation state is given by &minus;3 = &minus;6 + <em>x</em>, and this is an iron(III) complex. The name is potassium trisoxalatoferrate(III) (note that tris is used instead of tri because the ligand name starts with a vowel). Because oxalate is a bidentate ligand, this complex has a coordination number of six.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-38322">In this example, the coordination sphere has a cationic charge of 2+. The NH<sub>3</sub> ligand is neutral, but the chloro ligand has a charge of 1&minus;. The oxidation state is found by +2 = &minus;1 + <em>x</em> and is 3+, so the complex is pentaaminechlorocobalt(III) chloride and the coordination number is six.</li>
</ol>
</div></div></section>

