</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chemistry_1e_(OpenSTAX)/19:_Transition_Metals_and_Coordination_Chemistry/19.02:_Coordination_Chemistry_of_Transition_Metals" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="The_Structures_of_Complexes"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-38322 editable">The Structures of Complexes</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-38322">The most common structures of the complexes in coordination compounds are octahedral, tetrahedral, and square planar (Figure \(\PageIndex{7}\)). For transition metal complexes, the coordination number determines the geometry around the central metal ion. Table \(\PageIndex{3}\) compares coordination numbers to the molecular geometry:</p>

